  "slug": "heat-stress-95b0",
  "summary": "New preprints tie CCA1/LHY heat responses, potato clock phase and autumn-warming networks to regulators of temperature and stress responses.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is how regulatory layers set the timing and magnitude of plant responses to temperature and related stress. In Arabidopsis, CCA1 and LHY mutants show time-of-day heat responses across transcriptome, proteome and metabolome, implicating sulfur, ROS and related processes. In cultivated potato, diel expression phase differs from wild Solanum species independently of clock period, with DOF and REVEILLE transcription factors linked to these shifts. In white spruce, autumn warming decouples photosynthesis and cold-acclimation gene networks from growth cessation, with MYB and AP2/ERF regulators involved. Wheat m6A and metabolomic profiling adds an epitranscriptomic layer to drought, and MEAM-OWL offers motif-expression analysis validated on pearl millet. The new emphasis is on timing and decoupling of regulatory programs rather than the earlier priming and organ-level stress-combination focus.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
